This is actually a very interesting result, but it does not confirm a vulnerability.

What happened?

You sent:

curl.exe -vk http://iapp192644.croydon.ms.com:2376/version

The response was:

Received HTTP/0.9 when not allowed

This tells us:

The TCP connection to 2376 succeeded.
The service accepted the connection.
The service did not return a normal HTTP/1.1 response.
Therefore, the service is not a standard HTTP endpoint.

Port 2376 is commonly used for the Docker Engine Remote API over TLS. Since Nmap detected ssl/docker? and your HTTPS request produced TLS-related errors while your HTTP request produced an invalid protocol response, it is likely that this service expects TLS and possibly mutual TLS (client certificates).

This is not a vulnerability

You cannot report:

Docker Remote API exposed
Unauthenticated Docker API
Remote Code Execution

because you have not demonstrated access to the Docker API.

Next Step: Confirm TLS Requirement

If OpenSSL is available:

openssl s_client -connect iapp192644.croydon.ms.com:2376

or try:

curl.exe --http1.1 -vk https://iapp192644.croydon.ms.com:2376/version

If the server requests a client certificate or fails the handshake because one is missing, that indicates the API is protected.

